  <section class="compare-section compare-section--soft">
    <div class="compare-wrap">
      <span class="compare-kicker">Digital apparel options</span>
      <h2>Choose the method that fits the artwork and garment</h2>
      <div class="compare-methods">
        <article class="compare-card">
          <h3>What is DTG?</h3>
          <p>Direct-to-garment printing applies textile ink directly to the shirt. It is especially effective for cotton and cotton-rich garments when you want a soft print with full color, shading, gradients, or photo-quality detail.</p>
          <ul><li>No minimum order</li><li>Soft feel on cotton</li><li>Strong choice for photographs and detailed art</li></ul>
        </article>
        <article class="compare-card">
          <h3>What is DTF?</h3>
          <p>Direct-to-film printing creates the design on transfer film, then applies it with heat. It produces bright, durable artwork and works across more material types than a typical DTG project.</p>
          <ul><li>Cotton, polyester, and blends</li><li>Useful for shirts, compatible bags, and pressable hat styles</li><li>Full color with no minimum order</li></ul>
        </article>
        <article class="compare-card">
          <h3>Which is better for my order?</h3>
          <p>Choose based on the shirt fabric, graphic, placement, quantity, and deadline. DTG often wins for a soft photo print on cotton; DTF often wins when you need fabric flexibility, bright opacity, or unusual placement options.</p>
          <ul><li>Send the artwork first</li><li>Choose garments after discussing the process</li><li>Confirm timing before placing a rush order</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

